    Introduction
    LiNix is a cross-platform, multi-backend declarative package manager written in Rust. Unlike simple CLI wrappers, LiNix is a System State Engine designed for users who require Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) reliability on their local machines.

It ensures your system matches your defined configuration across Linux, macOS, and Windows, while providing transactional safety, recursive dependency logic, and cryptographically verified installations.

    Key Features
    ** 🛡️ Mission-Critical Reliability

    Transactional Journaling: Every sync is recorded in a journal (=pending.json=). If a sync is interrupted by a power failure or crash, the =heal= command resumes exactly where it left off.

    Atomic Snapshots: LiNix automatically creates a timestamped JSON backup of your declarative state before every operation, allowing for easy rollbacks.

    On-Disk Conflict Detection: Scans your =$PATH= before installation to ensure LiNix doesn't accidentally shadow or overwrite binaries you've manually compiled or installed outside the manager.

** 🚀 Advanced Logic

    Recursive Group Expansion: Groups can contain other groups (e.g., a "work" group that includes "dev" and "security" groups). LiNix resolves these using a queue-based expansion with infinite depth and cycle detection.

    Lockfile Support: Generates a =linix.lock= file capturing exact version strings. Use =linix sync --locked= to reproduce a system state down to the specific version on any machine.

    Backend Aliases: Map custom nicknames (e.g., =sys= -> =pacman=) to allow portable group files that work across different distributions.

** 🔐 Hardened Security

    SHA256 Verification: Verify the integrity of GitHub binary downloads using the =@sha256= option.

    Lua Hooks: Programmable hooks (before/after sync, before/after install) run in a sandboxed Lua environment for safe automation.

    Post-Install Health Checks: Automatically verify that a package's binary is present in the system =$PATH= immediately after installation using the =@verify_binary= option.

    Supported Backends
    LiNix provides mature, native implementations for the following managers:

Category	Backends
System	=apt=, =dnf=, =pacman=, =apk=, =zypper=
Universal	=flatpak=, =snap=, =brew=
Language	=cargo= (w/ =binstall= support), =npm=, =pip=, =pnpm=, =yarn=
Tools	=uv=, =mise=, =pipx=, =vscode=, =github=
Windows	=winget=, =choco=, =scoop=

    Installation
    ** From Source
    #+BEGIN_SRC bash
    git clone https://github.com/youruser/linix.git
    cd linix
    cargo build --release
    sudo cp target/release/linix /usr/local/bin/
    #+END_SRC

    Configuration
    ** Main Config (/.config/linix/config.toml)
    #+BEGIN_SRC toml
    enabled_backends = ["apt", "flatpak", "cargo", "github"]
    github_token = "ghp_your_token_here" # Higher rate limits

[aliases]
sys = "apt"

[groups]
base-dev = ["sys:git", "sys:curl", "sys:build-essential"]
rust-stack = ["base-dev", "cargo:ripgrep@verify_binary=rg"]
#+END_SRC

** Group Files (/.config/linix/groups/local.txt)
#+BEGIN_SRC text
rust-stack
github:neovim/neovim@sha256=a1b2c3d4...
Host-specific files (e.g. host-desktop.txt) are merged automatically

#+END_SRC

    Usage
    | Command | Description |
    | :--- | :--- |
    | =linix sync= | Sync system to match group files. |
    | =linix sync --locked= | Sync using exact versions from =linix.lock=. |
    | =linix heal= | Resume an interrupted or crashed sync session. |
    | =linix doctor= | Check health and availability of all backends. |
    | =linix rollback= | Restore group files from a previous snapshot. |
    | =linix clean= | Interactively remove packages not defined in config. |
    | =linix install <pkg>= | Install a package and save it to =local.txt=. |

    LiNix vs. Metapac
    While projects like Metapac provide an excellent CLI wrapper, LiNix is built for Mission-Critical stability:

    Resilience: LiNix has a =heal= command and transactional journals; Metapac is stateless.

    Security: LiNix supports native SHA256 checksums for GitHub downloads; Metapac relies on backend defaults.

    Logic: LiNix supports recursive group expansion (groups within groups); Metapac uses flat lists.

    Maturity: LiNix provides full support for =apk=, =choco=, and =pip=, which are currently on Metapac's wishlist.

